    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d916">

                <pb facs="0955" n="941" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg941"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.61351/8–2844: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Chargé in Cuba</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Nufer</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-08-28T20:00:00-04:00">August 28, 1944—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>704. At the first meeting with Cuban Sugar Commission, afternoon of August 28,
                    Mr. Wilson, CCC, stressed the excellent cooperation of the Cuban Sugar
                    Institute, the hope that these negotiations will cover the last global sugar
                    sale between the two Governments, and the basic nature of this Government’s
                    policy in maintaining the present sugar ceiling price.</p>
                <p>A United States proposal,<anchor n="35" xml:id="d916-f1"/> along the lines of the
                    enclosure to instruction no. 4412 of August 26,<anchor n="34" xml:id="d916-f2"/>
                    will be submitted to the Cuban Delegation on August 29.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d916-f1" n="35" xml:id="d916-f1-content">
                <p>The United States proposal (not printed), offering to purchase Cuba’s 1945 and
                    1946 sugar crops at a basic price of 2.65 cents per pound f.a.s., with an
                    escalator clause to protect Cuba from higher costs of production, was handed to
                    the Cuban delegation on August 29.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d916-f2" n="34" xml:id="d916-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
